From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ilya Vysotsky <il.mohoff2016@yandex.ru>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 03:49:17 +0400
Subject: [PATCH] The JS string builder asks for less in one piece on Windows
 10 Mobile

GrowEltsAggressively multiplies a string builder's space by eight until it
reaches 128 million elements. On a phone the 2 GB address space is in
pieces: an HP Elite x3 with Discord open had 350 MB free and nothing over
27 MB in one piece, and the policy turned strings of 4-15 million
characters into single requests of 64, 128 and 256 MB. 33 of the 46 failed
JS allocations in its logs were one extension's Array.join. Under
GECKO_W10M the eightfold growth stops at 4 million elements and doubling
takes over.

ExtractWellSized threw a finished string away when its shrink-to-fit
realloc failed: mozjemalloc shrinks a large block by making a new one and
copying, and there was no room for the smaller copy beside the original.
Under GECKO_W10M the string stays in the buffer it was built in.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 js/src/util/StringBuilder.cpp | 13 +++++++++++++
 js/src/util/StringBuilder.h   | 10 ++++++++++
 2 files changed, 23 insertions(+)

diff --git a/js/src/util/StringBuilder.cpp b/js/src/util/StringBuilder.cpp
index 08b54c99c6..41c721b4cb 100644
--- a/js/src/util/StringBuilder.cpp
+++ b/js/src/util/StringBuilder.cpp
@@ -39,12 +39,25 @@ static CharT* ExtractWellSized(Buffer& cb) {
   constexpr size_t minCharsToReclaim = 80 / sizeof(CharT);
   if (capacity - length >= minCharsToReclaim &&
       capacity - length > capacity / 4) {
+#if defined(GECKO_W10M)
+    // On Windows 10 Mobile a shrink that fails leaves the string in the
+    // buffer it was built in. For a large block mozjemalloc shrinks by making
+    // a new one and copying, and in an address space in pieces there is often
+    // no room for the smaller copy beside the larger original -- which threw
+    // away a finished string of 35 MB for want of a place to put 35 MB while
+    // 64 MB were held. The quiet realloc: the other one reports out of memory
+    // and collects before it gives up.
+    if (CharT* tmp = allocPolicy.maybe_pod_realloc<CharT>(buf, capacity, length)) {
+      buf = tmp;
+    }
+#else
     CharT* tmp = allocPolicy.pod_realloc<CharT>(buf, capacity, length);
     if (!tmp) {
       allocPolicy.free_(buf);
       return nullptr;
     }
     buf = tmp;
+#endif
   }
 
   return buf;
diff --git a/js/src/util/StringBuilder.h b/js/src/util/StringBuilder.h
index bad6ce196f..01c782cca4 100644
--- a/js/src/util/StringBuilder.h
+++ b/js/src/util/StringBuilder.h
@@ -29,7 +29,17 @@ namespace detail {
 // avoid very expensive memcpys for large strings (eg giant toJSON output for
 // sessionstore.js). Drop back to the normal expansion policy once the buffer
 // hits 128MB.
+#if defined(GECKO_W10M)
+// On Windows 10 Mobile once it hits 4 million characters. A 32-bit process has
+// 2 GB of address space, and on the phones it is in pieces: the largest free
+// one was 13-200 MB at the 33 failed allocations a uBlock Origin made there,
+// which this policy had turned into single requests of 64, 128 and 256 MB for
+// strings of 4-15 million characters. Doubling asks for a quarter to an
+// eighth of that, at the price of one more copy of at most the final size.
+static constexpr size_t AggressiveLimit = 4 << 20;
+#else
 static constexpr size_t AggressiveLimit = 128 << 20;
+#endif
 
 template <size_t EltSize>
 inline size_t GrowEltsAggressively(size_t aOldElts, size_t aIncr) {
